<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Covenanters</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Covenanters were Scottish Presbyterians who bound themselves by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-covenant">National Covenant</a> of 1638 and the Solemn League and Covenant of 1643 to defend their church against royal religious policy. Their revolt against Charles I began the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wars-of-the-three-kingdoms">Wars of the Three Kingdoms</a>, and a Covenanter army was sent to Ulster in 1642 to fight the Irish rebels. Persecuted after the Restoration, they remained a radical strand of Scottish Presbyterianism.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/covenanters.jpg" alt="Covenanters" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: MrPenguin20 · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Scottish_Covenanter_Flag.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The rising of 1641 began a decade of war involving the Catholic Confederation of Kilkenny, royalists, <b>Scottish Covenanters</b> and English parliamentarians, ended by Oliver Cromwell’s campaign of 1649–1650, with the storming of Drogheda and Wexford, and the completion of the conquest by 1653.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-ireland">Kingdom of Ireland · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In the 1640s the <b>Covenanting</b> army was among the strongest in Britain, but Oliver Cromwell crushed Scottish forces at Dunbar in 1650 and Worcester in 1651.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-scotland">Kingdom of Scotland · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Two years after his father's execution, Charles II was crowned at Scone by the Scottish <b>Covenanters</b>, the last coronation held there.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/january-1">1 January in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Covenanters</b>, Scottish Presbyterians, were persecuted after the Restoration.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/november-28">28 November in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The English Parliament and the Westminster Assembly accepted the Solemn League and Covenant with the Scottish <b>Covenanters</b>, already approved by the Church of Scotland.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/september-25">25 September in history</a></li></ul></section>
